#Step 1 — Create your folder and file

mkdir task-1
cd task-1


#Step 2 — Create a simple Node.js project

Inside task-1 terminal, run: npm init -y
This creates: package.json

Install testing and linting tools: npm install --save-dev jest eslint


#Step 3 — Create your application code

Create a file: touch app.js

Add the following onto the file:

function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

module.exports = add;

#Step 4 — Create a unit test

Create a file: app.test.js

Add into that file:

const add = require("../app");

test("adds two numbers", () => {
    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});

#Step 5 — Configure Jest

Open the package.json file.

Change the scripts section to show:


"scripts": {
    "test": "jest",
    "lint": "eslint ."
}

Now you can run:npm test

Expected: PASS app.test.js


Step 6 — Configure ESLint

Create another file: touch eslint.config.js

Add the following:

module.exports = [
    {
        files: ["**/*.js"],
        rules: {
            semi: "error",
            quotes: ["error", "double"]
        }
    }
];

Test linting: npm run lint


Step 7 — Create GitHub Actions workflow

If you do not have one already, create a directory for your workflows:
mkdir -p .github/workflows

Create a file: touch .github/workflows/ci.yml


Step 8 — Add CI pipeline

Open the yaml file: .github/workflows/yml

Add the following in:

name: Node.js CI Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

  pull_request:
    branches:
      - main


jobs:

  build:

    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:

      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4


      - name: Setup Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 24


      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm install


      - name: Run linting
        run: npm run lint


      - name: Run tests
        run: npm test


Step 9 — Test locally first

Run: npm run lint

Then run: npm test
Both should pass.


Step 10 — Push to GitHub

From task-1 directory:
Run the following:
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Create basic CI pipeline"

Connect your GitHub repository:
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR REPOSITORY.git

Push: git push -u origin main


Step 11 — Check GitHub Actions

Go to: GitHub Repository
        ↓
Actions tab
Node.js CI Pipeline

You should see:

✓ Checkout code
✓ Setup Node.js
✓ Install dependencies
✓ Run linting
✓ Run tests

You should also see a green tick.

